<Doc title="Terms of Service — Chatick" description="The terms you agree to when using Chatick." path="/terms">
  <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
  <p class="updated">Last updated: {UPDATED}</p>

  <p>
    These terms cover your use of Chatick — the website at <a href="https://chatick.com">chatick.com</a>, the web
    application at <a href="https://app.chatick.com">app.chatick.com</a>, and the desktop applications. By creating an
    account you accept them. If you disagree with any part, please do not use the service.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What Chatick is</h2>
  <p>
    Chatick is a team workspace: chat with an AI dispatcher, tasks, time tracking, documents, notes, files and
    resources. It is offered as-is, as a hosted service, and it is currently free to use.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Your account</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You sign in with Google. You are responsible for keeping access to that account secure.</li>
    <li>You must be old enough to enter a contract in your country, and at least 16.</li>
    <li>You are responsible for what happens in projects you own, including who you invite.</li>
    <li>One person, one account. Do not share credentials with people outside your team.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Your content stays yours</h2>
  <p>
    Everything you put into Chatick — messages, tasks, documents, notes, files — remains yours. We claim no ownership.
    We store and process it solely to provide the service to you and the people you share a project with.
  </p>
  <p>
    You keep the right to export your data at any time, and to delete it. Deleting a project or a company removes the
    associated content, including the stored files.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The AI dispatcher</h2>
  <p>
    Chatick sends the content of messages and related project data to a third-party language model provider in order to
    summarise, route and answer them. That processing is what makes the AI features work.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>AI output can be wrong.</strong> Treat it as a suggestion from a capable assistant, not as verified fact.
    Do not rely on it for legal, financial, medical or safety-critical decisions without checking it yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>Do not use Chatick to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>break the law, or help someone else break it;</li>
    <li>store or distribute malware, or attack any system;</li>
    <li>harass people, or publish content that is unlawful where you or your team live;</li>
    <li>upload material you have no right to upload;</li>
    <li>probe, overload or reverse-engineer the service, or work around its limits;</li>
    <li>resell the service as your own.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Public share links are a deliberate feature: anything you publish through one becomes readable by anyone holding the
    link, without signing in. Decide accordingly, and revoke links you no longer want live.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Free service, and what that means</h2>
  <p>
    Chatick is free while it is being built, with a storage allowance per project. Free does not mean unmanaged: we run
    backups and take reliability seriously. It does mean there is no paid support commitment and no uptime guarantee,
    and that limits may change as the service grows.
  </p>
  <p>If paid plans appear later, existing free projects will get clear notice before anything changes for them.</p>

  <h2>7. Availability and changes</h2>
  <p>
    We update Chatick continuously; features may be added, changed or removed. Every released version is described in
    the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>. We may suspend access temporarily for maintenance, and we may end the
    service — in which case you will be given reasonable notice and a way to export your data.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Suspension</h2>
  <p>
    We may suspend or close an account that breaks these terms, threatens the service, or harms other users. Where the
    situation allows it, we will explain why first.
  </p>

  <h2>9. No warranty, and limits of liability</h2>
  <p>
    Chatick is provided <strong>as-is and as-available</strong>, without warranties of any kind, to the fullest extent
    the law allows. We do not promise the service will be uninterrupted, error-free, or that it will fit a particular
    purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    To the fullest extent permitted by law, we are not liable for indirect, incidental or consequential damages, for
    lost profits, or for lost data beyond what our backups can restore. Nothing here limits liability that cannot be
